concept :the_ring_order do
  name "The Ring Order"
  tags :ecology, :military, :governance, :prediction
  prominence :recognized

  prose <<~PROSE
    A ring order condemns a stand of forest on #{ref :the_amur_line, "the Amur Line"}. A marking officer sets the compartment's number into the face of the post's hammer, strikes one stem in each corner and one at the centre, and the ring crew follows: a cut through bark and cambium the whole way round every woody stem, at 1.3 metres, the standard height for reading trunk diameter.

    Nothing is felled. A ringed compartment stands where it stood, grey by the second season, with its root plates undisturbed in the soil. Stems under 40 millimetres are cut at the collar and the faces painted with the same lime-and-oil paste that packs the rings, mixed dry at #{ref :tangwang_post, "the post"} because the line service puts nothing living into condemned ground.
  PROSE

  prose <<~PROSE, section: :mechanics, heading: "Three Sheets And The Board"
    A compartment is condemned on three sheets and no fewer. The coring record covers twenty increment cores taken on a forty-metre grid, all at 1.3 metres and all on the uphill side. It counts cores containing closed sleeves of foreign tissue; the reach's threshold is four. A sleeve the bench matches to a number off #{ref :the_control_series, "the control series"} is entered with that number and is not one of the four, because an ordinary partner fungus builds closed structures through outer rings as readily as anything the line is walking away from. #{ref :continuity, "Continuity"}'s deviation cabinet prints the second sheet after its ninth consecutive reading above threshold. It lists migration dates, the slopes on which deer have bedded, litter temperature at 100 millimetres and the hour the morning dew leaves, with its input record beneath them. A scout files the third sheet in the order the ground was walked.

    The three dates go up in chalk in three columns on the post's enamel board. The fourth names the marking officer who may lift the hammer from its peg. That officer cannot be responsible for the human sheet that first crossed its threshold. When the unattended cabinet opens a file, the post names one of the two human record keepers after the walk sheet arrives.

    When the core sheet and the deviation sheet name the same compartment in the same week, the walk sheet must carry the later date. The #{ref :the_others, "Others"} can drive fungal and machine systems to the same classification through different corrupted signals. The post therefore treats agreement as two readings, not as independent confirmation, until somebody has walked the ground.
  PROSE

  prose <<~PROSE, section: :operations, heading: "A Day In The Compartment"
    Compartments on the Xiao Hinggan reaches are cut to two hectares, the ground a crew of nine closes between light and light at midsummer. Ringing runs from full leaf to the solstice, when the bark lifts clean off the cambium and the day is long enough to finish. A file marked in autumn waits, and the band published with it is widened by the reach's measured advance in the interval — between six hundred and nine hundred metres in a year on the Tangwang.

    Every ringed stem takes the hammer's compartment number in the sapwood above the cut. A crew returning the following season reads the number to tell a stem the line killed from a stem that died on its own.

    An unclosed ring is neither a killed stem nor a recorded one, so a crew does not leave a compartment on a partial line. Work stops when the board's tally matches the hammer count. A compartment that cannot be closed in the day is refused and re-walked.
  PROSE

  prose <<~PROSE, section: :limits, heading: "The Reserved Band"
    Behind the ring runs a band two kilometres deep in which nothing may be cut, planted or lived in. Crews call it the засека, the belt, after the reserved forests of an older frontier where felling inside the line was a criminal matter. Its purpose on the register is a control: the coring staff need undisturbed wood for comparison, and a household's garden, firewood and worn biology make a control useless. The order reclassifies nothing by itself, and the compartment's #{ref :the_gradients, "gradient"} is entered as Altered when the band is published.

    Publication ends residence inside the band at the ring, which gives a settlement the days between the mark and the cut to move. #{ref :the_great_root, "The Great Root"} moves the settlement whole — charter name, clinic team, work rota, grave register — and sites each household so its garment reforms with the neighbours it already had. Wood may cross outward from a band under an open file if the marking officer allows it. Soil may not.

    #{ref :sanchakou, "Sanchakou"} is the settlement on the Tangwang whose orchard went with the compartment in front of it and whose burial rise stands inside the band, entered four times a year on the coring team's round. #{ref :the_sanchakou_ring, "The file that put it there"} was opened by the cabinet, refused by the cores and marked on a walk.
  PROSE

  log "2026-08-09 — Ring height, core height and the 40 mm inclusion limit come from forest mensuration, a trade nobody on the line practises: diameter is read at breast height, 1.3 m, so the ring, the core and the tally all sit at the one height a stem is already measured at."
  log "2026-08-09 — Felling is forbidden inside a ringed compartment on the strength of Job 14:7, 'there is hope of a tree, if it be cut down, that it will sprout again'. A cut stump on the line answers with sprouts from a root system already condemned, so the stand is killed standing and the root plate left in the soil undisturbed."
  log "2026-08-09 — The reserved band takes its depth-behind-the-line shape and its name from the Muscovite abatis belt, where cutting timber inside the reserved forest was punishable. Here the prohibition serves a measurement instead of an obstacle: undisturbed wood is what the coring staff compare against."
end
